<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Re-applying the baseline after a reboot is the only reason this app wants to hear about boot.
      Every other permission arrives with the phase that first calls something needing it, so the
      manifest can always be read as a list of what the code does rather than of what it might.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!-- The control-plane connection: enrollment, policy, heartbeat and the event stream. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- Read, never changed: the heartbeat reports whether this phone is on Wi-Fi or mobile data. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      The connection runs as a foreground service because the product's responsiveness claim rests
      on it: "lock now" must lock the phone, not queue until the next background window. See
      ConnectionService for why the type is `specialUse` and not `dataSync`.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
    <!--
      Android 13 and up. The service grants this to itself as device owner; it is declared because a
      permission that is not declared cannot be granted at all, and an ungranted one means the
      foreground-service notification is suppressed while the service keeps running — a managed phone
      with nothing on screen saying so.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Suspending an app requires knowing it is there. From Android 11 an app sees only the packages
      it declares an interest in, and the failure that causes here is silent in exactly the way this
      project keeps guarding against: `getInstalledApplications` returns a short list, every blocked
      package reads as "not installed", and the sync reports clean while the child keeps using the
      app. A device owner is documented as exempt from the filtering, but exempt-by-platform-policy
      is not something this code can assert, so the permission is declared as well and
      AppSuspensionManager checks that its own package is in the result before believing any of it.

      This app is installed from a provisioning QR and never from Play, so the Play policy that
      restricts this permission to a short list of app categories does not gate it — and device
      administration is on that list regardless.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
      Screen time (FR-3) is measured from `UsageStatsManager`, and this is the declaration that makes
      the grant possible at all — without it this app does not appear in Settings → Special app
      access → Usage access and `appops` has nothing to allow.

      Declaring it is NOT granting it. `PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS` is an appop, and no device-owner API can
      set one: `setPermissionGrantState` covers runtime permissions only. The grant is a one-time
      step per device, recorded in DEPLOYMENT.md. Until it happens every query returns nothing, so
      the reader answers "not measured" rather than zero — a zero would make the daily limit
      unreachable and show the parent a child who spent the day off their phone.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS"
        tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" />

    <!--
      Bedtime has to start on a phone nobody is holding. The engine already computes the instant at
      which the answer changes; without an alarm nothing acts on it, and the device re-evaluates only
      while the screen is on — so a phone put down at 20:30 with a 21:00 bedtime enforces nothing at
      21:00, and the child picks it up to an unrestricted phone.

      Declared, then checked at run time: this is an appop, not a plain permission, and an app
      targeting Android 13 or later does not hold it by default. AlarmManagerPlatform asks
      `canScheduleExactAlarms()` and falls back to a wake-up the platform may delay rather than to
      none, so an ungranted appop costs minutes of precision instead of the whole feature.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />

    <!--
      The find-my-phone siren (FR-9) rings on the alarm stream and vibrates with it. The sound needs
      no permission; the vibration does. A lost phone is often a silenced phone, and the pulse is what
      carries through a sofa cushion — dropping it would leave the feature working only in a quiet
      room, which is not where a phone goes missing.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      "Locate now" (FR-9). Both are declared because from Android 12 a request for FINE alone is
      answered with COARSE when the user chooses approximate location, and an app that never declared
      COARSE gets nothing at all in that case — a parent looking for a child would see "no position"
      on a phone whose GPS is working.

      BACKGROUND is what makes it work when it matters. The command arrives over the event stream
      while the phone is in a pocket, and without it every fix would come back only if the child
      happened to have the app open — which this app has no UI to be open in. The service's
      foreground type stays `specialUse` on purpose: adding `location` to a service that starts at
      boot risks ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException on Android 14, which would cost the
      connection itself rather than one command.

      All three are runtime permissions, so the device owner grants them to itself at startup — see
      ConnectionService.grantOwnPermissions. Declaring is not granting, and LocationProbe reports "not
      permitted" as itself rather than as "no position".
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />

    <application
        android:name=".FamilyGuardApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.FamilyGuard">

        <!--
          Exported, because the system's device-policy service is what sends these broadcasts, and
          it is a different process. `BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN` is what keeps that from meaning "any app on
          the phone": it is a signature-level permission held only by the platform, so an exported
          component guarded by it is reachable by the system and by nothing else.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".admin.AdminReceiver"
            android:description="@string/admin_description"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/admin_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.device_admin"
                android:resource="@xml/device_admin" />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.DEVICE_ADMIN_ENABLED" />
                <action android:name="android.app.action.PROFILE_PROVISIONING_COMPLETE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          Binding this service is how the platform treats the owner app as persistent. Without it an
          OEM task killer can stop the DPC and the phone silently enforces nothing until the next
          boot — a failure whose only symptom is that a bedtime does not happen.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".admin.AdminService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.DEVICE_ADMIN_SERVICE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          The two activities the setup wizard drives during QR provisioning. Same reasoning as the
          receiver: exported because the caller is the system, and guarded so that is all it means.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".provisioning.GetProvisioningModeActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.FamilyGuard.Invisible">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.GET_PROVISIONING_MODE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <activity
            android:name=".provisioning.PolicyComplianceActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.FamilyGuard.Invisible">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.ADMIN_POLICY_COMPLIANCE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The offline escape hatch (FR-12), and the only component on this phone a person can start.

          Exported, unavoidably and on purpose: a MAIN/LAUNCHER activity is reachable by the launcher
          and therefore by anything, and it cannot carry a permission — a signature permission would
          make the launcher unable to start it, which is to say it would make the escape hatch
          unreachable in exactly the situation it exists for. So the protection is not on the
          component. It is that this activity accepts no input except the code typed into it: it
          reads no Intent extras, no data URI and no action, and every decision it makes comes from
          the encrypted stores. `ManifestAndPlatformCallsTest` names this component as the one
          permitted exception and asserts that property, rather than taking this comment's word.

          A launcher entry rather than a deep link or a notification tap, because the notification
          exists only while ConnectionService runs, and a service that is not running is one of the
          reasons somebody is standing here.

          `adjustResize` so the keyboard shrinks the scrolling column instead of covering the button.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".recovery.RecoveryActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.FamilyGuard"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The long-lived connection. Not exported: nothing outside this app has any reason to start
          or stop it, and the three components that do — the compliance activity, the admin receiver
          and the boot receiver — are all in this package.

          `specialUse` rather than `dataSync`, which is what this looks like. From Android 15 a
          `dataSync` service is capped at six hours in any twenty-four, so the connection would die
          every evening and come back the next morning — arriving with a targetSdk bump rather than
          with a code change, and showing up only as bedtimes that stopped happening.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".sync.ConnectionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Continuous device-policy enforcement for a fully managed device: holds
                    the control-plane event stream open so a parent's change takes effect in seconds
                    rather than at the next background window." />
        </service>

        <!--
          Not exported. BOOT_COMPLETED is a protected broadcast that only the system can send, so
          there is nothing an app on the phone could gain by reaching this — and nothing it should
          be able to try.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".admin.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

    </application>

</manifest>
